														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4445628">
									

<p>I think you've built a good foundation for the two cops for sure, but a little more flavor wouldn't hurt.</p>
<p>I think in this situation you could have a quick, effective cascade of breaking tone. First they're chummy and &quot;lets get this done&quot; sort of informal, but once they see the decrepid state of the home and smell the blood, that tone breaks way to a more formal, defensive tension of &quot;cop mode&quot;. Declaring they're entering, stating they have a warrant, pulling guns, etc. Then, after trying to put down the dog and being confronted by the anomaly, that tone breaks as well, and they sink into revulsion and fear.</p>
<p>Also, if these are supposed to be simple townie cops, the idea that the dialogue would close with &quot;we've got a find a way to put these things down&quot; seems&#8230; odd. Like, if they aren't Foundation, they should be far more disturbed and confused by these events. Solution seeking when confronted with the anomalous is more a Foundation response.</p>

							</div>

								
